{
  "image_name": "1c17182af0080d84bcdc3a1574d9148c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three nanoparticle-based strategies for generating tumour-targeting CAR macrophages in or around solid tumours. In all cases, the goal is to deliver genetic material encoding a tumour-specific CAR to macrophages so that they become CAR-macrophages.\n\nThe top scheme shows intravenous injection of lipid nanoparticle–formulated mRNA. These particles circulate to the tumour microenvironment, are taken up by macrophages, and provide mRNA that drives CAR expression on the macrophage surface. The middle scheme uses polymer-formulated DNA delivered by intratumoural injection. Here, a DNA nanocarrier reaches local tumour-associated macrophages, leading to intracellular DNA and mRNA and ultimately CAR display. The bottom strategy places a nanoparticle–hydrogel depot into the tumour bed after tumour resection. This depot appears to slowly release genetic cargo and added immune enhancers, promoting local macrophage programming into CAR-macrophages.\n\nAcross the figure, the comparison emphasizes different delivery routes—systemic, direct intratumoural, and post-surgical local depot—but a shared mechanism: nonviral nanocarriers reprogram macrophages within the tumour setting to express tumour-specific CARs. The image highlights localized immune engineering rather than ex vivo cell manufacturing.",
